 ## Overview
-The THREAD System is an early surface extraction method that reconstructs 3D geometry from a sequence of 2D cross-sectional images by tessellating between outlines on consecutive slices [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing internal structures (such as medical anatomy) by creating a polygonal mesh from segmented 2D boundaries, differing from grid-based isosurface extraction methods like Marching Cubes by operating directly on discrete slice contours rather than a volumetric scalar field [S1, unofficial].
+The THREAD System is an early isosurface extraction method that constructs 3D surfaces from a sequence of 2D cross-sectional outlines. It solves the problem of visualizing volumetric data by tessellating between adjacent slice contours and applying surface-normal shading [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from modern grid-based methods (like Marching Cubes) by its reliance on explicit 2D boundary detection and inter-slice interpolation rather than implicit scalar field evaluation on a 3D grid [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
